            <ArticleTitle>[Chronic sciatic pain caused by sciatic nerve schwannoma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>748-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.revmed.2008.01.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Schwannomas are tumors with slowly progressive symptoms, more often painful than associated with neurologic deficits. Involvement of the sciatic nerve mimics chronic sciatic pain due to a herniated disk. If there is no lumbar pain and lumbar MRI is normal, the sciatic nerve must be clinically and radiologically examined all along its course. We report a patient with chronic sciatica in whom physical examination revealed a painful mass with a Tinel sign in her back thigh. MRI showed a multilobular mass along the sciatic nerve. Surgery of this plexiform schwannoma allowed a complete relief from pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
